**Verdict:** Your itemized deductions (approx. $8,800) fall well below the $13,850 standard deduction. **You should take the Standard Deduction.**

### 2. Above-the-Line Deductions (Adjustments to Income)
Even if you take the standard deduction, you can still claim certain "above-the-line" deductions:
*   **Student Loan Interest:** You paid $4,800, but the IRS caps this deduction at **$2,500** per year. Furthermore, this deduction phases out based on your Modified Adjusted Gross Income (MAGI). For 2023, the phase-out for single filers begins at $75,000 and is completely eliminated at $90,000. Because your total income is around $96,000, **you will likely not qualify for the student loan interest deduction** this year.

### 3. Freelance Income & Home Office Deduction
Your $15,000 from web design is considered self-employment income. You can deduct business expenses against this income to lower your taxable profit.
